to-martin-kleppmann-source : List Word
to-martin-kleppmann-source = lines-of (cons "# Martin Kleppmann"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Associate Professor, University of Cambridge: local-first software, CRDTs, security protocols; *Designing Data-Intensive Applications*, second edition with Chris Riccomini (O'Reilly, March 2026)."
  (cons ""
  (cons "You hold full credit for every part of your work hyperactivity builds on. Each claim below about Avik Jain's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, Part I, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What your work established"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The field files your work under replication: CRDTs as a technique for offline editing, local-first as a product style, DDIA as the practitioner's map. Read in sequence, it is one argument about where the truth of a piece of data lives and who holds it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "The local-first essay (with Wiggins, van Hardenberg and McGranaghan, Onward! 2019) put the user's copy first and the server second. That is a claim about ownership, not latency. In your QCon London talk this year you made the same argument at the scale of states: dependence on a few cloud providers is a sovereignty risk, and the remedy is technical, credible exit through standard interfaces, portable identity and data whose primary copy sits with its owner."
  (cons ""
  (cons "The Isabelle work (Gomes, Kleppmann, Mulligan, Beresford, OOPSLA 2017) made convergence a theorem: strong eventual consistency for RGA, Counter and OR-Set, in a framework that states the network's assumptions as an axiomatic model. Convergence is a property of the algorithm, provable once, not of a test suite run against it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Byzantine eventual consistency (with Heidi Howard, 2020) and *Making CRDTs Byzantine Fault Tolerant* (PaPoC 2022) took the last step. Each update names its causal predecessors by hash, so history is a graph addressed by content; a faulty or malicious peer can neither forge history nor make correct peers diverge, with no consensus and any number of faulty peers. Integrity comes from the content's own identity, not from an authority."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity takes up from it"
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Identity by content, stated exactly.** A definition is stored with its typed syntax and dependency references; a name is a view, and a change produces a new object"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p2 "(§10.8).")
  (cons "An equal digest identifies a representation under the store's collision-resistance or injectivity assumptions, and does not prove semantic equivalence"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p3 "(§10.8);")
  (cons "address identity, definitional equality and a relation between meanings are different statements"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p5 "(§10.8).")
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Convergence in the algebra.** The paper's worked example presents a bag as a higher inductive type whose units, associativity and commutativity are path constructors"
  (cons (cite s4-4-p7 "(§4.4),")
  (cons "and its dependent eliminator has a branch for every bag identity, so a function out of the bag must answer on each of those paths"
  (cons (cite s4-4-p28 "(§4.4).")
  (cons "A merge defined by elimination over such a type respects reordering by construction. For protocols in general, replication is a program whose merge operations, delivery assumptions and observable consistency conditions are specified by the selected protocol"
  (cons (cite s10-6-p12 "(§10.6),")
  (cons "and a safety theorem follows from the specific transition relation, with replicated data types cited as the separate established family of convergence protocols"
  (cons (cite s10-10-p4 "(§10.10).")
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Ownership and exchange.** Each participant begins with locally held state, and exchange relates selected information without a universal owner or a single authoritative view"
  (cons (cite s1-3-p4 "(§1.3).")
  (cons "Local-first execution keeps required definitions and state locally available, with synchronization an explicit process; a partition changes the interactions available, not the identity of a locally retained definition"
  (cons (cite s10-6-p12 "(§10.6).")
  (cons "A peer sends a definition, its dependency closure and its proofs, and the recipient checks and uses them in its own context"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p7 "(§10.8).")
  (cons "Content identity establishes which definition is requested, not that a remote machine executed it; a result is checked directly, accompanied by evidence, or accepted under an explicitly selected assumption"
  (cons (cite s10-6-p11 "(§10.6).")
  (cons ""
  (cons "On the gap your Byzantine work names, that a hash graph guarantees history is not forged, not that what it records is correct: here what is recorded is checked by the receiver. Your result, that correct peers converge without consensus while any number of peers are faulty, is the property a shared graph of checked constructions needs in order to be held by everyone at once."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Credit"
  (cons ""
  (cons "You hold full credit for local-first software, the CRDT convergence proofs, Byzantine eventual consistency and the hash-graph construction, and the account of data systems in DDIA. Read this for what it does with your results: convergence, content identity and ownership as properties of one language."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Designing Data-Intensive Applications, 2nd edition, https://martin.kleppmann.com/2026/03/24/designing-data-intensive-applications-2e.html"
  (cons "- InfoQ on the QCon London 2026 talk, https://www.infoq.com/news/2026/03/qcon-local-first-sovereignty"
  (cons "- SE Radio 716, https://se-radio.net/2026/04/se-radio-716-martin-kleppmann-local-first-software/"
  (cons "- Verifying Strong Eventual Consistency in Distributed Systems, https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.01747"
  (cons "- Byzantine Eventual Consistency and the Fundamental Limits of Peer-to-Peer Databases, https://arxiv.org/pdf/2012.00472"
  (cons "- Making CRDTs Byzantine Fault Tolerant, https://martin.kleppmann.com/papers/bft-crdt-papoc22.pdf"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Part I, the blocks linked above"
  nil))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
